From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 15:30:38 +0200
Subject: dt-bindings: net: add the Intel xRX500 packet DMA controller

Four instances carry the traffic of the two switch macros. Describe them
as what they are, a register window and an interrupt with no channel
cells: a transmit channel is tied to a dequeue port of the packet buffer
manager and fetches its descriptors from the window the manager stages
them in, so there is nothing an arbitrary consumer could ask for.

The thirteen properties that tune an instance in the vendor device tree
are not carried over. They set the arbitration mode, the bus burst
length, the channel polling interval, descriptor placement, prefetch and
byte enables, and one of them is a software queue budget with no
register behind it at all. No board file of the vendor's overrides any
of them, and none of them can be board data, because they describe how
the SoC wires its own fabric. They belong in the driver, keyed on the
instance, where a value that reaches no register is visible as one.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/net/lantiq,xrx500-dma.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/net/lantiq,xrx500-dma.yaml
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..e9943d296777
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/net/lantiq,xrx500-dma.yaml
@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0-only OR BSD-2-Clause)
+%YAML 1.2
+---
+$id: http://devicetree.org/schemas/net/lantiq,xrx500-dma.yaml#
+$schema: http://devicetree.org/meta-schemas/core.yaml#
+
+title: Intel xRX500 packet DMA controller
+
+maintainers:
+  - Grische <github@grische.xyz>
+
+description: |
+  One of the four packet DMA controllers of the Intel MIPS xRX500 (GRX500)
+  SoC family. Each carries one direction of one GSWIP-3.0 switch macro's
+  traffic, and its channels are hard-bound: a transmit channel is tied to a
+  dequeue port of the packet buffer manager and fetches its descriptors from
+  the window the manager stages them in, and a receive channel writes into a
+  ring in memory. The controller offers nothing to arbitrary consumers, so it
+  is not a dmaengine provider and has no channel cells; a driver reaches it
+  through the phandle of the endpoint that uses it.
+
+  Everything else about a controller -- its arbitration mode, bus burst
+  length, channel polling interval, descriptor placement and prefetch, and
+  the endianness of the descriptors it fetches -- is fixed for each of the
+  four instances by how the SoC wires it up, and belongs to the driver rather
+  than to the device tree.
+
+properties:
+  compatible:
+    const: lantiq,xrx500-dma
+
+  reg:
+    maxItems: 1
+
+  interrupts:
+    maxItems: 1
+    description:
+      Aggregate interrupt of the controller. A channel's own completion
+      interrupt is reported through it, and the driver reads the pending
+      channel from the controller's registers.
+
+required:
+  - compatible
+  - reg
+  - interrupts
+
+additionalProperties: false
+
+examples:
+  - |
+    #include <dt-bindings/interrupt-controller/irq.h>
+    #include <dt-bindings/interrupt-controller/mips-gic.h>
+
+    dma@1c100000 {
+            compatible = "lantiq,xrx500-dma";
+            reg = <0x1c100000 0x10000>;
+            interrupt-parent = <&gic>;
+            interrupts = <GIC_SHARED 67 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+    };
